                    <node index="0" text="Flint uses Android's Accessibility service to detect which app is in the foreground using app and screen class names, and read the web address from known browser address bars for website blocking. Not all browsers or pages expose an address. In the browsers Flint supports, while a website rule is active, Flint also re-reads the address bar when a page changes without opening a new tab. Android sends page-change signals from other apps too; Flint ignores those without reading their content. After setup, the list of known browsers is on Flint's You health screen. Address-bar URLs stay on this device, in memory for blocking; they are not saved as browsing history. Websites you add to a rule remain in your rules. Address-bar reading stops when Accessibility is off. Flint uses these reads to show its block screen when your app or website rules apply." resource-id="" class="android.widget.TextView" package="com.flint.peakfocus" content-desc="" checkable="false" checked="false" clickable="false" enabled="true" focusable="false" focused="false" scrollable="false" long-clickable="false" password="false" selected="false" visible-to-user="true" bounds="[190,191][975,1317]" drawing-order="0" hint="" display-id="0" />
